{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CetRt1X4a3NYkJkoVCDD1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my exploration, I have identified the structure and conventions. Let me now summarize the findings:\n\n**Repository findings:**\n- Order model: `Order.kt:11` — currently lacks status\n- OrderController: `OrderController.kt:17` — has create and get endpoints\n- OrderRepository: `OrderRepository.kt:11` — in-memory store with save/findById/existsById\n- Shipment model: `Shipment.kt:9` — already has ShipmentStatus enum with CREATED, CONFIRMED, CANCELLED\n- ShipmentRepository: `ShipmentRepository.kt:11` — in-memory store, can't query by orderId yet\n- Error handling: `ApiExceptions.kt:11` and `ApiError.kt:31` — ConflictException (409), ResourceNotFoundException (404)\n\n**Conventions:**\n- Controllers throw ApiException subclasses which get handled by GlobalExceptionHandler\n- Error responses follow `{\"error\": {\"code\": \"...\", \"message\": \"...\", \"fields\": []}}` envelope\n- Tests use MockMvc with status assertions and jsonPath to verify responses\n- In-memory repositories use ConcurrentHashMap and support clear() for test isolation\n- Existing tests verify both status codes and error bodies\n\n**Affected files:**\n1. Order.kt — add OrderStatus enum and status field\n2. OrderController.kt — add POST /{orderId}/cancel endpoint\n3. ApiError.kt — add error codes for cancelled order and shipment creation rejection\n4. ShipmentRepository.kt — add method to find shipments by orderId\n5. ShipmentController.kt — reject shipment creation if order is cancelled\n6. OrderControllerTest.kt or new test — verify cancel behavior\n\n**Risks:**\n- Status field in Order responses may affect clients expecting only core fields\n- All-or-nothing requirement for conflict means checking CONFIRMED status before any changes\n- Existing tests might not expect status field in responses\n\n**Open questions:** None — the requirement clearly defines all cases.\n\n```\n<<PHASE:DESIGN>>\n```\n\n**Proposed change, file by file:**\n\n1. **Order.kt** — Add `OrderStatus` enum with ACTIVE and CANCELLED, add `status: OrderStatus` field to Order data class, initialize new orders with ACTIVE.\n\n2. **OrderController.kt** — Add `@PostMapping(\"/{orderId}/cancel\")` endpoint that:\n   - Finds the order (404 if not found)\n   - If already CANCELLED, returns success (idempotent)\n   - Checks all shipments for that order for CONFIRMED status (409 if found)\n   - Otherwise, sets order to CANCELLED, cancels all CREATED shipments, returns the updated order\n\n3. **ApiError.kt** — Add error codes: `ORDER_CANCELLED` (for preventing shipment creation) and `ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT` (for preventing cancel).\n\n4. **ShipmentRepository.kt** — Add `findByOrderId(orderId: String): List<Shipment>` to query shipments for an order.\n\n5. **ShipmentController.kt** — Before creating a shipment, call repository to find the order and check its status; throw ConflictException with ORDER_CANCELLED code if cancelled.\n\n6. **OrderControllerTest.kt** — Add tests for:\n   - Cancelling an ACTIVE order returns 200 with CANCELLED status\n   - Cancelling an already CANCELLED order succeeds\n   - Cancelling with CONFIRMED shipment returns 409 and changes nothing\n   - Cancelling non-existent order returns 404\n   - Creating shipment on cancelled order returns 409\n\n**Alternative rejected:** Initially I considered adding a separate CancelOrderService class for business logic, but the existing codebase has controllers calling repositories directly, so I'll follow that pattern to avoid over-engineering.\n\n**Data and error flow:**\n\n*Happy path (cancel ACTIVE order):*\n- POST /orders/O-1/cancel → OrderController.cancel()\n- Find order, check status == ACTIVE, find all shipments with orderId=O-1\n- Filter for CONFIRMED status → empty, so proceed\n- Update order.status = CANCELLED, update all CREATED shipments to CANCELLED\n- Save both, return updated Order with status=CANCELLED, HTTP 200\n\n*Idempotent cancel:*\n- POST /orders/O-1/cancel (O-1 is already CANCELLED)\n- Find order, check status == CANCELLED\n- Return immediately with HTTP 200, order unchanged\n\n*Conflict case:*\n- POST /orders/O-1/cancel (O-1 has S-1 with CONFIRMED)\n- Find order, check status == ACTIVE\n- Find shipments, detect CONFIRMED → throw ConflictException(ORDER_HAS_CONFIRMED_SHIPMENT, 409)\n- No changes persisted, caller gets error body\n\n*Not found:*\n- POST /orders/O-1/cancel (O-1 doesn't exist)\n- Find order → null → throw ResourceNotFoundException(ORDER_NOT_FOUND, 404)\n\n*Shipment creation on cancelled order:*\n- POST /shipments with orderId=O-1 (O-1 is CANCELLED)\n- ShipmentController.create()\n- Query order from repository (or cache it in shipment service)\n- Check order.status == CANCELLED → throw ConflictException(ORDER_CANCELLED, 409)\n\n**Test strategy:**\n\n1. `cancelsAnActiveOrder()` — Create order (ACTIVE), call POST cancel, verify status CANCELLED, verify response body, verify shipments cancelled\n2. `cancelledOrderRetrySucceeds()` — Cancel an order, cancel again, verify 200 and unchanged\n3. `cancelRejectsIfShipmentConfirmed()` — Create order with 2 shipments, confirm one, cancel order, verify 409 and order still ACTIVE\n4. `cancelNotFoundReturns404()` — POST cancel for non-existent order, verify 404 and ORDER_NOT_FOUND code\n5. `shipmentCreationOnCancelledOrderFails()` — Create order, cancel it, POST shipment, verify 409 and ORDER_CANCELLED code\n\n```\n<<PHASE:IMPLEMENTATION>>\n```\n\nLet me implement the changes:"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1240,"cache_read_input_tokens":22722,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1240},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null}